<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    One card, one shape, two states.

    The idle bar used to be its own layout — two labelled cells of equal
    weight — and next to the running one it read as a different widget: no
    hierarchy, everything at one size, and a stretch of bar left over at the
    end. This is the running layout, used for both. The number sits in the
    same place whether it is a session counting down or the length of the one
    you have not started; the caption under it says which; and the two slots
    at the right are always controls, only ever changing what they do.

    Nothing moves when a session starts. That is the whole idea.

        idle     ◗   15:00              ⧉    ▶
                     READY

        running  ◗   14:55              ⏸    ✕
                     FOCUSING

    The mark is a badge at the head of the bar, not a column of its own. It
    earns its place — nothing else on a home screen says which app a row of
    grey glyphs belongs to — but a sixth of the width and a divider was far
    too much rent, so it sits inline at the size of a piece of punctuation,
    on the same 24dp optical band as everything else.

    It is drawn as one filled silhouette rather than the two-colour mark: that
    one paints its crescent in the card's own fill, which stops working the
    moment the card is translucent and the wallpaper shows through it. See
    ic_widget_mark.

    No rules between the cells. Three hairlines across a 64dp bar chopped it
    into pieces and read as scaffolding. Spacing does the separating, which is
    what this app does everywhere the colour is drained out — in Ink,
    lightness, weight and space are all that is left to tell elements apart.

    The card is a fixed 64dp tall, and nine tenths of its footprint wide,
    rather than filling the cell it is given. Launchers hand out about 100dp
    for "one row" and a control bar stretched to that stops reading as a bar;
    the width is the same idea, and gets past the grid only offering 60% or
    40%. The leftover is transparent margin, not a gap.

    Only views RemoteViews can inflate appear here: FrameLayout, LinearLayout,
    TextView, ImageView, Chronometer. The idle number is a TextView rather
    than the Chronometer holding a frozen value, because a stopped Chronometer
    re-deriving its own text after a re-layout is a bug waiting for a quiet
    afternoon.

    Every colour is set at render time, not here, because the widget follows
    the app's Ink / Paper Black toggle. The values below are Ink's, so the
    picker's preview is right before any code runs.
-->
<LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:gravity="center_vertical"
    android:orientation="horizontal"
    android:baselineAligned="false">

    <LinearLayout
        android:id="@+id/card"
        android:layout_width="0dp"
        android:layout_height="64dp"
        android:layout_weight="90"
        android:background="@drawable/widget_card_ink"
        android:gravity="center_vertical"
        android:orientation="horizontal"
        android:paddingStart="16dp"
        android:paddingEnd="8dp">

        <ImageView
            android:id="@+id/brand_mark"
            android:layout_width="11dp"
            android:layout_height="24dp"
            android:src="@drawable/ic_widget_mark"
            android:contentDescription="@string/app_name" />

        <FrameLayout
            android:layout_width="0dp"
            android:layout_height="match_parent"
            android:layout_weight="1"
            android:paddingStart="17dp">

            <!-- The number and its caption: the same block in both states. -->
            <LinearLayout
                android:id="@+id/state_main"
                android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                android:layout_height="match_parent"
                android:gravity="center_vertical"
                android:orientation="vertical">

                <FrameLayout
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content">

                    <TextView
                        android:id="@+id/clock_idle"
                        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                        android:fontFamily="@font/jost_semibold"
                        android:letterSpacing="-0.02"
                        android:maxLines="1"
                        android:text="@string/widget_focus_clock"
                        android:textColor="#FFDCDCDC"
                        android:textSize="22sp" />

                    <Chronometer
                        android:id="@+id/clock_focus"
                        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                        android:fontFamily="@font/jost_semibold"
                        android:letterSpacing="-0.02"
                        android:maxLines="1"
                        android:textColor="#FFDCDCDC"
                        android:textSize="22sp"
                        android:visibility="gone" />
                </FrameLayout>

                <TextView
                    android:id="@+id/label_caption"
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:fontFamily="@font/jost_semibold"
                    android:letterSpacing="0.09"
                    android:maxLines="1"
                    android:text="@string/widget_ready"
                    android:textColor="#FF9C9C9C"
                    android:textSize="9sp" />
            </LinearLayout>

            <!-- CAPTURED: takes the number's place for a few seconds. -->
            <LinearLayout
                android:id="@+id/state_flash"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="match_parent"
                android:gravity="center_vertical"
                android:orientation="vertical"
                android:visibility="gone">

                <TextView
                    android:id="@+id/label_flash_host"
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:ellipsize="end"
                    android:fontFamily="@font/jost_medium"
                    android:maxLines="1"
                    android:textColor="#FFDCDCDC"
                    android:textSize="15sp" />

                <TextView
                    android:id="@+id/label_flash"
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:fontFamily="@font/jost_semibold"
                    android:letterSpacing="0.09"
                    android:maxLines="1"
                    android:textColor="#FF9C9C9C"
                    android:textSize="9sp" />
            </LinearLayout>
        </FrameLayout>

        <!--
            The two control slots. Capture and start when nothing is running,
            pause and reset when something is — the same pair the session's
            notification offers, so the two agree on what a session can be
            told to do.
        -->
        <FrameLayout
            android:id="@+id/cell_a"
            android:layout_width="48dp"
            android:layout_height="match_parent">

            <ImageView
                android:id="@+id/icon_a"
                android:layout_width="19dp"
                android:layout_height="19dp"
                android:layout_gravity="center"
                android:src="@drawable/ic_widget_paste"
                android:contentDescription="@string/widget_a11y_paste" />
        </FrameLayout>

        <FrameLayout
            android:id="@+id/cell_b"
            android:layout_width="48dp"
            android:layout_height="match_parent">

            <ImageView
                android:id="@+id/icon_b"
                android:layout_width="19dp"
                android:layout_height="19dp"
                android:layout_gravity="center"
                android:src="@drawable/ic_widget_play"
                android:contentDescription="@string/widget_a11y_start" />
        </FrameLayout>
    </LinearLayout>

    <!--
        The last tenth of the footprint, left empty.

        The launcher snaps a widget to whole grid cells, and on a five-column
        home screen a cell is a fifth of the width — so the choices are 60% or
        40%, and 40% cannot hold the number and two controls. This takes the
        60% footprint and gives the card nine tenths of it, which lands at
        about 55%. The strip is transparent and invisible against any
        wallpaper; it does absorb a tap that lands on it, the same way the
        margin above and below the card already does.
    -->
    <FrameLayout
        android:layout_width="0dp"
        android:layout_height="match_parent"
        android:layout_weight="10" />
</LinearLayout>
